    {"answer": "A", "reason_rejected": "Friction is a force, not a property of the cargo that directly causes the time difference in this context; the primary variable changing is mass/momentum. While friction might vary slightly, the dominant factor for stopping distance/time at same speed is inertia/momentum."},
    {"answer": "C", "reason_rejected": "Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity. The heavy ship actually has *less* deceleration (magnitude of negative acceleration) for the same force, but the question asks what *causes* the longer time. The cause is the larger quantity of motion (momentum) that needs to be removed."},
    {"answer": "D", "reason_rejected": "Constant velocity is a state of motion, not a cause for stopping time differences. Both start at constant velocity, but the heavy one takes longer to change that state."}
